\subsubsection*{3.2 For-loop Desugaring}

\texttt{for x in c: body} is equivalent to:

\begin{verbatim}
it = iter(c)
while True:
    try:
        x = next(it)
    except StopIteration:
        break
    body
\end{verbatim}

This explains why \texttt{for} works on generators, lists, files, and
instances of user classes alike: they all supply an \texttt{\_\_iter\_\_}
method. It also explains why a one-shot iterator behaves differently
from a re-iterable container when looped over twice.
